          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong CW-complex
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nice+topological+space"} nice
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            which is, or can be, built up inductively, by a process of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/attaching+space"} attaching
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-disks"} n-disks
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^n
            along their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary"} boundary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-spheres"} (n-1)-spheres
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^{n-1}
            for all
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cell+complex"} cell complex
            built from the basic topological cells
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ↪
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^{n-1} \hookrightarrow D^n
          %p
            Being, therefore, essentially combinatorial objects, CW complexes are the principal objects of interest in
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalent
            to CW-complexes. Notably the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+realization"} geometric realization
            of every
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-groupoid"} 2-groupoid
          %p
            Milnor has argued that the category of spaces which are homotopy equivalent to CW-complexes, also called
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/m-cofibrant+spaces"} m-cofibrant spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nice+category+of+spaces"} convenient category of spaces
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
          %p
            Also, CW complexes are among the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrant+objects"} cofibrant objects
            in the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+topological+spaces"} classical model structure on topological spaces
            %em every
            topological space is
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalence"} weakly homotopy equivalent
            to a CW-complex (but need not be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} strongly homotopy equivalent
            to one). See also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CW-approximation"} CW-approximation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+object"} fibrant object
            in this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            structure, this means that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            on the CW-complexes is a category of “homotopically very good representatives” of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            and
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+hypothesis"} homotopy hypothesis
            for more on this.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              %strong (origin of the “CW” terminology)
            %p
              The terminology “CW-complex” goes back to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Henry+Constantine+Whitehead"} John Henry Constantine Whitehead
              (and see the discussion in
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "#HatcherTopologyOfCellComplexes"} Hatcher, “Topology of cell complexes”, p. 520
            %p To quote from the original paper, which was “an address delivered before the Princeton Meeting of the (American Mathematical) Society on November 2, 1946”, Whitehead states:
            %blockquote
              %p
                In this presentation we abandon
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+complexes"} simplicial complexes
                in favor of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cell+complexes"} cell complexes
            %p Thus the “CW” stands for the following two properties shared by any CW-complex:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %strong C
                  \= “closure finiteness”: a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+subset"} compact subset
                  of a CW-complex intersects the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interior"} interior
                  of only finitely many cells (
                  %a{:href => "classical+model+structure+on+topological+spaces#CompactSubsetsAreSmallInCellComplexes"}> prop.
                  ), hence in particular so does the closure of any cell.
              %li
                %p
                  %strong W
                  \= “weak topology”: Since a CW-complex is a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
                  in
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
                  over its cells, and as such equipped with the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/final+topology"} final topology
                  of the cell inclusion maps, a subset of a CW-complex is open or closed precisely if its restriction to (the closure of) each cell is open or closed, respectively.
            %p
              (Whitehead called the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interior"} interior
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-disks"} n-disks
              the “cells”, so that their closure of each cell is the corresponding
              = succeed "-disk.)" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            %br/
          %h2#Definition Definition
          %p
            In the following let
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convenient+category+of+topological+spaces"} convenient category of topological spaces
          #SpheresAndDisks.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              %strong (spheres and disks)
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              write
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi D
                          %mi n
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi Top
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^n \in Top
                  for the
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-disk"} n-disk
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphism
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+ball"} closed unit ball
                  in the
                  = succeed "-dimensional" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean space
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
                  and equipped with the induced
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subspace+topology"} subspace topology
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo −
                            %mn 1
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi Top
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^{n-1} \in Top
                  for the
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-sphere"} (n-1)-sphere
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary"} boundary
                  of the
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-disk"} n-disk
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subspace+topology"} subspace topology
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi i
                          %mi n
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo −
                            %mn 1
                        %mo ↪
                        %msup
                          %mi D
                          %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_n \;\colon\; S^{n-1} \hookrightarrow D^n
                  for the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
                  that exhibits this
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary"} boundary
                  inclusion.
                %p
                  We also call these functions the
                  %em generating cofibrations
                  (of the
                  = succeed ")." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+topological+spaces"} classical model structure on topological spaces
            %p Notice that
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mn 1
                        %mo =
                        %mi ∅
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^{-1} = \emptyset
                  ;
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mn 0
                        %mo =
                        %mo *
                        %mo ⊔
                        %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^0 = \ast \sqcup \ast
                  \.
          #SingleCellAttachment.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              %strong (single cell attachment)
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              and for
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                \-cell
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/attaching+space"} attachment
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is the result of gluing an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-disk"} n-disk
              to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-sphere"} (n-1)-sphere
              (def.
              = succeed "):" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SpheresAndDisks"}
            %p Let
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\phi \;\colon\; S^{n-1} \longrightarrow X
            %p
              be a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/attaching+space"} attaching space
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo ∪
                      %mi ϕ
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mi n
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    X \cup_\phi D^n \,\in Top
            %p
              is the topological space which is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout"} pushout
              of the boundary inclusion of the
              = succeed "-sphere" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} diagram commute
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi S
                              %mrow
                                %mi n
                                %mo −
                                %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mi ϕ
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi ι
                                    %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi po
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi D
                              %mi n
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                            %msub
                              %mo ∪
                              %mi ϕ
                            %msup
                              %mi D
                              %mi n
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    S^{n-1} &amp;\stackrel{\phi}{\longrightarrow}&amp; X
                    \\\
                    {}^{\mathllap{\iota_n}}\downarrow &amp;(po)&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    D^n &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; X \cup_\phi D^n
                    }
                    \\,.
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %div{:style => "float:right;margin:0 10px 10px 0;"}
              %img{:src => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/GluingHemispheres.jpg", :width => "400"}/
            %p
              If we take the defining boundary inclusion
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota_n \colon S^{n-1} \to D^n
              itself as an attaching map, then we are gluing two
              = succeed "-disks" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^{n-1}
              = succeed "-sphere:" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi S
                              %mrow
                                %mi n
                                %mo −
                                %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi i
                                  %mi n
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi D
                              %mi n
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi i
                                    %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi po
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi D
                              %mi n
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi S
                              %mi n
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    S^{n-1} &amp;\overset{i_n}{\longrightarrow}&amp; D^n
                    \\\
                    {}^{\mathllap{i_n}}\downarrow &amp;(po)&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    D^n &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; S^n
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p (graphics from Ueno-Shiga-Morita 95)
          .num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              A single cell
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/attaching+space"} attachment
              of a 0-cell, according to def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SingleCellAttachment"}
              is the same as forming the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union+space"} disjoint union space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⊔
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \sqcup \ast
              with the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point"} point
              space
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msup
                              %mi S
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                %mn 1
                            %mo =
                            %mi ∅
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                                %mo ∃
                                %mo !
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi po
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msup
                              %mi D
                              %mn 0
                            %mo =
                            %mo *
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                            %mo ⊔
                            %mo *
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    (S^{-1} = \emptyset) &amp;\overset{\exists !}{\longrightarrow}&amp;  X
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;(po)&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    (D^0 = \ast) &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; X \sqcup \ast
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p
              In particular if we start with the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+topological+space"} empty topological space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %mi ∅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = \emptyset
              itself, then by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/attaching+space"} attaching
              0-cells we obtain a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+topological+space"} discrete topological space
          #CellAttachments.num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              %strong (attaching many cells at once)
            %p
              If we have a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              of attaching maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mi i
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mi i
                    %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{S^{n_i-1} \overset{\phi_i}{\longrightarrow} X\}_{i \in I}
              (as in def.
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SingleCellAttachment"}
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union+space"} disjoint union space
              of their
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain"} domain
              spheres
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
